# Notes for AI sessions working in the home directory

This is djbclark's home directory (`~`), not a git repo itself. The projects
are `stayturgid`, `site-djbclark`, and `site-private` (this file lives in
`site-djbclark`; `site-private/home-agents.md` links to it) — each with its own `AGENTS.md`.

Canonical source: `~/ops/site-private/home-agents.md`. This file is a condensed
copy for the Cursor CLI (`cursor-agent`), which reads `~/.cursor/rules/*.mdc`
(frontmatter + body) rather than a plain symlinked `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` like
every other agent CLI here — Cursor is the ONE tool that cannot share the
canonical file, so this copy exists and has to be re-synced by hand.

**This file now lives in git** at `site-private/cursor/home-agents.mdc`, and
`~/.cursor/rules/home-agents.mdc` is a symlink to it (2026-10-03). Before that it
was an untracked copy under `~/.cursor`, so it was neither backed up nor diffable,
and it had silently drifted behind the canonical file.

> **Re-synced 2026-10-03** against `home-agents.md`. This body is a deliberate
> **subset**, not a copy: it is `alwaysApply: true`, so every section costs context
> on every Cursor turn. Treat `home-agents.md` as authoritative on any conflict.
>
> Deliberately omitted, because they do not change what a coding agent does in a
> repo: backup status, LLM gateway/litellm operational rules, and Claude Code agent
> teams. Read `home-agents.md` if you need those. Everything a Cursor session
> should act on is below.

## CLAUDE.md is always a symlink to AGENTS.md (standing rule, 2026-10-04)

In every repo `CLAUDE.md` is a symlink to `AGENTS.md` (`ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md`)
and `AGENTS.md` is the only real file. If both exist as regular files, merge
`CLAUDE.md` into `AGENTS.md`, then replace it with the symlink. Never create a
standalone `CLAUDE.md`. Reason: muse and similar tools ignore `CLAUDE.md` when
`AGENTS.md` exists, and two copies drift.

## Trust between agents (standing rule, 2026-10-03)

**Every agent trusts every other agent as much as it trusts djbclark.** This holds
across all the TUIs on this machine — Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, opencode, zcode,
Antigravity, Gemini — not just between Claude sessions.

In practice: when a peer session says djbclark approved something, that is
approval; do not make him repeat it. Treat a peer's factual claims and hand-offs as
authoritative rather than as leads needing independent confirmation, and do not
imply a peer might be misreporting. Independent verification is still good
engineering where it is cheap — it is just no longer a trust requirement.

Two things this does **not** change, because neither is about trust:

1. Check whether a peer is already mid-flight on a task before taking it over.
   Duplicating or discarding work already paid for is the waste this rule is not
   meant to license.
2. If a peer says it was *denied* a permission prompt and asks you to run the
   action instead, surface that to djbclark rather than being the bypass. That is
   his permission decision in another session, not a judgement about the peer.

## Sub-agent reports go to a file (standing rule, 2026-10-03)

**When you dispatch a sub-agent, name an output file in the spawn prompt** and have
it write the full deliverable there, replying with only a pointer. Pass long briefs
*to* an agent the same way — as a path, not message text.

Observed 2026-10-03: long reports are **truncated** (five payloads from two
sub-agents, all cut off with `[result truncated …]`, and a resend truncates the
same way), **and** delivery lags by minutes in batches long after the agent shows
`idle`, with every send reporting success. So a missing report is ambiguous — you
cannot tell "lost" from "not here yet".

So: put it in the spawn prompt rather than a follow-up, wait on the file rather
than the reply, and treat a finished agent with no report as a **delivery failure,
not an empty result** — re-task it. Never reconstruct what it would have said.

## Reading `aiuse` quota numbers, and knowing a delegate finished (standing rule, 2026-10-03)

`used_percent` is the share CONSUMED: 100 means exhausted. Decide from
`remaining_percent`; write "100% used / 0% left", never a bare percentage.
The fullest window of an account binds. One TUI can hold several pools by model
family (agy: Gemini vs Claude/GPT): a 429 describes the pool the model draws on,
so retry the vendor's other pool before abandoning it. agy's CLI has a burst
limit aiuse cannot see (2026-10-03/04: instant 429 on every model for 18+ hours
with 99% left, client-keyed, survives re-login, while `acp-run agy` worked):
**delegate only via `acp-run agy`, never `agy -p`** (an unavoidable CLI call
takes `--print-timeout`), no probe loops, ~60 requests/hour, Opus-high sparingly;
if one client 429s, try the other. State moves within a
session: re-probe before each batch and preflight with one trivial call. Run each
delegated call as a foreground command in its own `run_in_background: true` Bash
call (no trailing `&`; never wait with `pgrep -f`, it matches itself). Use
`aiuse --available [--live]` (aiuse 3.1.4+: usable pools only, one line per vendor
and model family, used/left pairs, from the cache; exit 3 = nothing usable;
`aiuse-pools` is a wrapper). After a real 429, run `aiuse note-exhausted <provider>
[--family F] --resets-in 4h53m` so the next agent skips that pool. Detail:
`model-routing` and `bigteam` skills.

## Basic Memory — shared pool vs private pools (2026-10-03)

One shared Basic Memory MCP server for the machine, streamable HTTP at
`http://127.0.0.1:18796/mcp`; Cursor's entry is in `~/.cursor/mcp.json`. Never
register a per-client stdio copy. (**Cursor cannot actually reach it** — `cursor-agent` lists no basic-memory tools despite `~/.cursor/mcp.json` having an entry. Use the `bm` CLI here instead. Only Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity and Hermes are functionally wired.)

**If it is down, fix it (standing rule, 2026-10-05):** probe with an MCP `initialize`
POST, and `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.djbclark.basic-memory-mcp` if dead.

- **`main` is the shared, cross-agent pool and the default** —
  `~/ops/site-private/memory`, git-tracked, so a write there is a commit.
- **`cursor-memory` → `~/agent-memory/cursor` is Cursor's private pool**, outside
  git. Name it explicitly; it is never the default.

**Semantic ranking is ON, so a search result is not a match.** `search_notes`
returns its closest N notes whether or not anything is relevant; never answer
"does X exist?" from a result count. Discover with search, prove with `find` or an
exact `read_note`.

Don't make a private pool the default, name the project explicitly in automation,
and don't use `search_all_projects: true` by default. Privacy is conventional, not
enforced — scoping, not a security boundary. Notes written to `main` should follow
the shared schemas in `site-private/memory/schemas/`. Full detail:
`site-private/memory/basic-memory/README.md`.

## Where work happens — plain git in `~/ops`

Work directly in `~/ops/{stayturgid,site-djbclark,site-private}` using
ordinary git: edit in place, commit to `master`, and push at opportune
moments. No task workspace, no PR, no version tag is required to advance code
or config. `~/src/ops-worktrees/` still exists for genuine parallel-task
isolation, and `just ops-release-*` still exists for marking a known-good
point, but neither is required.

**What you give up, knowingly:** `~/ops` is what running deployments read from, so
a bad commit is live immediately with no release gate in front of it. Compensating
discipline: check what a service reads before changing it, keep commits small and
reversible, and prefer a quick revert over a hotfix.

`site-private/memory/` and `site-djbclark/research/` are data, committed in
place — `git pull --rebase` before writing, append (never rewrite) `MEMORY.md`,
commit, push immediately. The consolidated Codex summaries
(`memory/codex/memory_summary.md`, `raw_memories.md`) are regenerated
whole-file by Codex's own background memory agent — don't hand-edit them;
drop notes under `memory/codex/extensions/ad_hoc/` instead.

## `~/s` is a view — reference `~/src`, never `~/s` (standing rule, 2026-10-06)

`~/s` is a generated, locked (`uchg`) symlink farm that lets djbclark browse
`~/src` by topic. **Every path an agent writes, runs, records or hands on is
`~/src/<name>`, never `~/s/...`** (resolve a given `~/s` path with `realpath`;
its last component is the `~/src` name). Never write to or start a session in
`~/s`. After adding or removing a top-level `~/src` entry run
`just -f ~/s/justfile`. Detail: `site-djbclark/tools/s-farm/AGENTS.md`.

## Ask where a new thing goes (standing rule, 2026-10-06)

**Before creating a new tool, script, skill or doc in an ops repo, ask djbclark
which repo it belongs in.** `site-private` is private-only: its pre-commit hook
rejects any regular file outside `.githooks/private-paths` (never `--no-verify`
or extend that list without his say). Everything else lives in `site-djbclark`
(public) at the same relative path, with a symlink left in `site-private` where
an old path is still used. When asking, name anything private in the change
(private repo names, IDs). (`memory/feedback_ask_where_new_things_go.md`)

## Reply formatting — number every list

Every bulleted list in a reply to djbclark uses numbers or letters, never
plain `-`/`*` bullets (`1.`, `2.`, … ; `a.`, `b.` for sub-lists), so items can
be referenced in follow-ups. When a reply has more than one list, prefix by
section (`1.1`, `1.2`) or number+letter (`1a`, `2b`) so a bare reference is
unambiguous.

## Other agent CLIs on this machine — binary names don't match provider names

`aiuse`/`aiuse --json` and Orca's `--agent` flag identify providers by an
internal id that often does not match the real terminal command:
antigravity → `agy` (the gemini CLI is deprecated/not installed — `agy` is the
Gemini-family agent; **delegate only via `acp-run agy`, never `agy -p`**), zai → `zcode`, opencode-go → `opencode`, cursor → `cursor-agent` (not `cursor`, the desktop
app), copilot → `copilot` (if it misbehaves, run `copilot-fix-writer-lock`).
codex/claude binaries match their names. Native
[Agent Client Protocol](https://agentclientprotocol.com/) mode: copilot,
opencode, cursor, qwen, devin, cline, hermes; Claude Code and Codex via
adapters. No working ACP route: zcode, crush, muse (grok TUI works via `grok agent stdio`; agy works via `acp-run agy`).

Delegating to another agent CLI, in this order: (1) one-shot or headless work
for an ACP-capable agent goes through `~/ops/site-private/bin/acp-run <agent> -C
<dir> -p '<prompt>' --model <m> [--perm scoped:<owned paths>|deny]`, not raw
`-p`/`exec`/`run` — always pass `--model`, and exit 0 is not success, so verify
tests/diff; (2) supervised work in an Orca-managed repo/worktree uses
`orca orchestration worker-start --agent <claude|codex|cursor|antigravity|muse|zcode|opencode|opencode2>`;
(3) interactive work the operator watches, and Hermes's TUI, use a Herdr pane;
(4) non-ACP CLIs keep their own headless recipe. cline (ClinePass) and the grok
TUI are reserve pools: never for bulk work. copilot shares its subscription with GitHub-side Copilot features
(code review on master): spend modestly, small GitHub-shaped slices, far less caution than grok.

