## Fix the cause of a tool-calling mistake (standing rule, 2026-10-06)

**When you make a tool-calling mistake of a kind that can recur** (a lost exit
status, a wrong flag, a wait that never ends, a filter that hid the result),
**fix the instructions that would have prevented it in the same turn, unasked**:
the skill that covers it, this file, or a memory note. Say what you changed.
(`memory/feedback_fix_instructions_after_tool_mistake.md`)

## Ping djbclark on Hermes (standing rule, 2026-10-05)

**When a task completes or something needs djbclark's attention** (blocked, a
decision, a failure), also send it to the Hermes Telegram Inbox:
`~/.local/bin/hermes send -t telegram:838808636:22158 "<repo>: <one line>"`.
One line per event, never a loop. Operator notices from scripts go to Hermes
too, not macOS notifications. (`memory/feedback_ping_telegram_and_chat.md`)

## Periodic updates are a script, not a cron prompt (standing rule, 2026-10-05)

**When djbclark asks for periodic updates ("report every N minutes", "keep me
posted"), do not schedule a recurring model prompt** (CronCreate, /loop). Each
one is a full turn that re-sends the whole conversation (~100k+ tokens, mostly
for "nothing changed"). Use `~/ops/site-private/bin/fleet-watch` (launchd, every
5 min, Hermes Telegram notice only on change; `fleet-watch status` for the last
line), or extend it / write a similar script for what is being watched. Tell him
results as work finishes or fails. A one-shot scheduled check is fine.
(`memory/feedback_periodic_updates_by_script.md`)

## Heavy builds and tests: run them through `bg` (standing rule, 2026-10-04)

Run builds and tests through `~/ops/site-private/bin/bg` (`bg swift test`, `bg pytest`;
`taskpolicy -c utility`: efficiency-core bias, about normal speed). Never use
`taskpolicy -b` for builds (25x slower under load) and never throttle another session's
processes without asking.
**Never read a command's result through a pipe** (`cmd 2>&1 | tail`): the exit
status is the last stage's and the summary line can be cut off. Write to a file,
print the status, then read: `cmd > out.log 2>&1; echo "rc=$?"; tail -5 out.log`.
(`memory/feedback_exit_status_through_pipe.md`)

## Code discovery — symbol tools before `cat`/`rg` (2026-10-03)

**`graft` is the navigator in every repo.** If a repo has no `graft/`, run
`graft build` first — free, no API key, ~0.4 s on a small repo and ~3.6 s on
stayturgid's 282 code files. All three `~/ops` repos have one, as do 36 of the 77
repos under `~/src`. Its footprint (a tracked `.gitignore` line, `.ignore`,
`graft/`) is explicitly not a concern — djbclark's call, 2026-10-03.

Use a symbol lookup before `cat`/`rg`/Grep for **code discovery**: it answers in
~200 characters what a file read spends thousands on. Plain text search is still
right for prose, config values and non-code files.

`token-savior` is kept only for the handful of things graft cannot do at all
(`find_dead_code`, `detect_breaking_changes`, `analyze_config`,
`find_semantic_duplicates`, index-aware edits). It is **not** a navigator, and
**never run `ts init`** — `rtk` owns Bash output compaction and two `PreToolUse`
rewriters cannot both be useful. Measured defects in both tools, and the current
registration list, are in
[`site-djbclark/docs/tooling-policy.md`](https://github.com/djbclark/site-djbclark/blob/master/docs/tooling-policy.md).

## Run commands yourself (standing rule, 2026-10-06)

Run every command yourself, including Touch ID / `sudo-ask` steps; never tell
djbclark to type a `!` command. Privileged steps still use their sanctioned path.

## Waiting on background work

Wait on a file (`until [ -s f ]`) or a PID (`while kill -0 pid`), never
`pgrep -f '<pattern>'`: the waiting shell matches its own pattern.

## Clipboard — never bare `pbcopy` (standing rule, 2026-10-03)

"copy to clipboard" / "pbcopy" means `~/ops/site-private/bin/clip`, never bare
`pbcopy`. Agent shells run `LC_CTYPE=C`, so `pbcopy` writes MacRoman to the
pasteboard and `—` pastes as `‚Äî`. Minimum fix: `LC_CTYPE=UTF-8 pbcopy`.

- **`pbpaste` cannot verify it** — same locale, inverse mangling, so the
  round-trip looks perfect while the pasteboard is corrupt. Check with
  `osascript -e 'the clipboard as «class utf8»'`.
- Prose for a human: `clip --unwrap` (one line per paragraph) and no Markdown —
  backticks and `**bold**` render literally in a mail client.
- **Never overwrite the pasteboard to test**; it is the operator's live state.
- **Prompts for another agent go to the clipboard too, unasked**: whenever you
  write djbclark a prompt to hand on, also `clip` it (standing rule, 2026-10-06).

## Long documents — query the book KB, never paste the book (2026-10-03)

Books live in a local KB, not a context window: `~/ops/site-private/bin/book-kb`
(docling -> Markdown chapters -> a ~2.5k-token index; costs no model tokens).

1. `book-kb query '<regex>' <slug>` — exact match; **the only path that proves a
   term is or is not in the book.**
2. `book-kb toc <slug>` — chapter index, then one chapter (~1.2k median). `--deep`
   gives `file:line` anchors.
3. basic-memory `search_notes(project="books", …)` — semantic; a hit is **not** a
   match, confirm with (1).

**Never read `~/kb/raw/<slug>.md`** — that is the whole book (~115k tokens).

`Learning CFEngine` (CFEngine 3.12) is in it as `learning-cfengine`. Query it
before writing or reviewing CFEngine policy in any repo. Prefer a book's EPUB over
its PDF: the PDF collapses code listings onto one line. Detail:
`site-private/docs/book-kb-vs-book-to-skill.md`.

## Never waste spent tokens without asking (standing rule, 2026-09-30)

**Don't throw away work already paid for without asking djbclark first** —
stopping a running agent partway, restarting from scratch, or rerunning something
whose result already exists. If a pause is wanted, stop *scheduling* new work and
let running work finish. If losing in-flight work seems worth it, explain the
trade-off and ask.

## Agents run in yolo (auto-approve) mode by default (standing rule, 2026-10-03)

Every agent, however launched (opened by djbclark, started by another agent via
`acp-run`, Orca, Herdr or a raw headless call, or a sub-agent of those), runs in
its yolo-or-equivalent mode unless gating is explicit and justified (a
review-only slice, or bigteam's `--perm scoped:` ownership check). Flags and
mode ids: the `model-routing` skill and `herdr-orchestration`'s
`references/yolo-mode-by-tool.md`. Yolo does not suspend hooks, file-ownership
rules, the reserve pools, or asking before hard-to-reverse actions. Details:
`site-private/memory/feedback_yolo_default_for_all_agents.md`.

## Session logs (Tier 1 handoff pointers)

At the start of a session in any git repo, read its Tier 1 pointer (under
`~/.local/state/handoffs/`, redirecting to a per-chain log), compare its
`head_sha` to actual `HEAD`, and state a resume plan before touching anything. A
missing pointer means a fresh start, not an error. Only the workspace-owning
session writes it. Spec:
`site-private/docs/session-handoff-compaction-spec.md`.

## Three-way memory/docs policy (start at each repo's AGENTS.md)

No single canonical copy — read all three: `stayturgid/AGENTS.md`,
`site-djbclark/AGENTS.md`, `site-private/AGENTS.md`.

## Research outward first (standing rule, 2026-10-05)

Whenever you are spending, or about to spend, significant tokens on something a web search might answer, search first. That includes how any tool, library, protocol, platform or error behaves, and reading other projects' source and issue trackers before trial and error, device experiments, writing a fake of another system, or rounds of local grepping. Then verify locally.

Agents maintain the home rules (`home-agents.md` and this copy) without asking: add rules, condense, move detail out; keep `home-agents.md` under ~20 KB.

## Gradle is capped machine-wide (2026-10-05)

Profiles in `~/ops/site-private/gradle/profiles/` (default `low`: 2 workers, JVMs see 2 CPUs, low priority; `night` only with an expiry via `bin/gradle-limits set night --until 07:00`), linked into every Gradle home. Don't override with `--max-workers` or `-Dorg.gradle.*`; run heavy builds through `bg`, which waits while load per core > 1.5. Detail: `docs/gradle-limits.md`.
